<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Notarisation requires the hardened runtime, and under it a process
	     may not run code it wrote into its own memory unless it says so
	     here. LuaJIT does exactly that, and the world is Lua, so it starts
	     doing it immediately.

	     Both keys, because they cover two different ways of asking.
	     allow-jit is the narrow one and covers pages mapped with MAP_JIT;
	     the LuaJIT Homebrew ships does not use MAP_JIT at all - it maps
	     anonymous read-write pages and turns them executable with
	     mprotect afterwards - and allow-unsigned-executable-memory is what
	     covers that. The narrow one is kept for the day LuaJIT changes.

	     Getting this wrong is quiet rather than loud: the refusal stops
	     the compiler, not the game, so it still starts and still plays,
	     only interpreted, and a smoke test would not notice.

	     Mind the wording in here: codesign parses this file with a
	     stricter reader than plutil does, and a pair of hyphens inside a
	     comment - illegal in XML, tolerated nearly everywhere else - is
	     rejected as a syntax error. Do not write option names with their
	     leading dashes. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
